course: history
lesson: 39
title: Canada: a short story
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: For thousands of years Canada has been home to First Nations and Inuit peoples, with many languages and ways of life; later the Métis, of mixed First Nations and European roots, grew up in the fur trade, and all three are still a big part of the country today.
explain: In 1608 the French founded Québec and traded furs with First Nations by canoe across huge distances; beaver fur for European hats was so valuable that the beaver became a symbol of Canada.
explain: In 1759 the British won the Battle of the Plains of Abraham and France gave up Canada, but French Canadians kept their language, which is why a Canadian cereal box has English on one side and French on the other.
explain: Canada became its own country step by step and peacefully, starting with Confederation in 1867, and a railway finished in 1885 tied it together from ocean to ocean.
explain: It also has a painful story, the residential schools, for which Canada has apologised, and today it calls itself a mosaic of cultures under the red maple leaf.
fact: Canada has been home for thousands of years to First Nations and Inuit peoples, with many languages and ways of life, and the Métis people, of mixed First Nations and European roots, grew up later during the fur trade.
fact: French explorers and settlers founded Québec in 1608 and traded furs with First Nations across the country.
fact: Beaver fur was in great demand for European hats, and traders and First Nations trappers travelled huge distances by canoe, so the beaver became a symbol of Canada.
fact: In 1759 the British won the Battle of the Plains of Abraham at Québec, and France gave up Canada, but French Canadians kept their language, religion and laws.
fact: Canada has two official languages, English and French.
fact: In 1867 several colonies joined to form the Dominion of Canada, which is celebrated every year on Canada Day, 1 July.
fact: Canada became independent step by step and peacefully, not through a revolution.
fact: The Canadian Pacific Railway, finished in 1885, linked the east to British Columbia on the Pacific, and thousands of Chinese workers did the most dangerous work.
fact: For over a hundred years many Indigenous children were taken from their families to residential schools, where they were forbidden to speak their own languages and many were treated badly; the last schools closed only in the 1990s, so many who went there are alive today.
fact: Canada has apologised for the residential schools and is working towards reconciliation.
fact: Canadian soldiers fought in both World Wars, for example at Vimy Ridge in 1917 and on D-Day in 1944, which gave Canada a stronger sense of being its own nation.
fact: Canada's red maple leaf flag was adopted in 1965.
fact: Canada welcomes immigrants from all over the world and calls itself a mosaic of cultures, and Toronto is one of the most diverse cities on Earth.
exercise: Who are the Indigenous peoples of Canada? | First Nations, Inuit and Métis | Aztecs and Inca | Māori and Aboriginal Australians | Vikings and Normans
exercise: Who founded Québec in 1608? | The French | The British | The Spanish | The Dutch traders
exercise: Why was beaver fur so valuable? | It was used for fashionable hats in Europe | It was eaten as a tasty treat at royal feasts in Paris and London | It was used to build houses | Beavers were very rare pets
exercise: How did fur traders travel across Canada? | By canoe | By train in 1608 | By motor car | By aeroplane
exercise: What happened at the Battle of the Plains of Abraham in 1759? | The British won, and France later gave up Canada | The French won and kept all of North America for themselves | Canada became independent | The Vikings landed
exercise: What did French Canadians keep after 1759? | Their language, religion and laws | Nothing at all | Only their castles | Their king
exercise: What are Canada's two official languages? | English and French | English and Spanish | French and Ukrainian | English and Inuktitut only
exercise: What does Canada Day on 1 July celebrate? | Confederation in 1867 | The end of the First World War | The founding of Québec | The Moon landing
exercise: How did Canada become independent? | Step by step and peacefully | Through one great revolution | By buying itself from France | It is still a British colony
exercise: When was the railway across Canada finished? | 1885 | 1608 | 1965 | 1759
exercise: Who did much of the most dangerous work on the railway? | Thousands of Chinese workers | British lords | Knights | Nobody, machines did it all
exercise: What happened in residential schools? | Indigenous children were taken from families and forbidden their languages | Children went there freely to learn their own languages and the old stories of their people | Only adults went there | They were summer holiday camps
exercise: What has Canada done about the residential schools? | Apologised and worked towards reconciliation | Nothing at all, it has kept quiet about them | Built more of them | Forgotten them completely
exercise: Where did Canadian soldiers fight in 1917? | Vimy Ridge | Hastings in England | Gallipoli only | Botany Bay in Australia
exercise: When was the maple leaf flag adopted? | 1965 | 1867 | 1759 | 2000
exercise: What does Canada call itself because of its many cultures? | A mosaic of cultures | A melting pot of knights | A castle of nations | An island of beavers
test: Ollie from Ohio asks why Canadian cereal boxes have French on them | Canada has two official languages, English and French, from its French history | The cereal is made in Paris and shipped all the way across the Atlantic to Canada | French is the only language in Canada | It is a mistake by the factory
test: The USA broke away from Britain with a war. How was Canada's road different? | Canada became independent step by step and peacefully | Canada fought a longer war | Canada never became independent | Canada was conquered by the USA
test: Why is the beaver on Canadian things like the five-cent coin? | The fur trade made the beaver a symbol of Canada | Beavers built the dam beside the very first Canadian parliament | It was the king's pet | It is a joke from 2020
test: What would a country stretching thousands of kilometres need to hold it together in 1885? | A railway linking east and west | A bigger castle | More beavers | A wall around it
test: A child sent to a residential school was not allowed to speak her own language. Why is that so painful? | It cut children off from their families and culture | It made school easier, because every lesson was in one language | It was what families wanted | It only lasted one day
test: eh, why'd canada say sorry for the schools? isn't that old history | The harm affected many families still alive, and apologising helps healing | Canada did not say sorry | Because the schools were too far away from the cities and cost a lot of money to run | It was a joke
test: Why did fighting at Vimy Ridge matter to Canadians so much? | It gave Canada a stronger sense of being its own nation | It was the first battle ever fought by any army anywhere in the world | Canada lost Québec there | It ended the Second World War
test: A Toronto classroom has kids whose families come from 20 countries. What does that show? | Canada is a mosaic of many cultures | Toronto is a school for travellers | Only one culture lives in Canada | Kids move every year
test: Who lived in Canada before the French arrived in 1608? | First Nations and Inuit peoples, for thousands of years | Nobody, it was empty land with only beavers, moose and bears | Only the British | Only Vikings
test: Why did the Chinese railway workers deserve to be remembered? | They did the most dangerous work building the railway | They owned the railway | They designed the maple leaf flag | They fought at Vimy Ridge
exam: Identify the event commemorated annually upon the first day of July in Canada. | The Confederation of eighteen hundred and sixty-seven | The founding of Québec in sixteen hundred and eight | The Battle of the Plains of Abraham | The adoption of the maple leaf flag
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which Canada attained independence? | Gradually and by peaceful means | By a single violent revolution | By purchase from France | It has never attained independence
exam: Ascertain the official languages of Canada. | English and French | English alone, everywhere | French and Spanish together | English and Latin equally
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the residential schools of Canada. | Indigenous children were removed from their families and forbidden their own languages | They were established at the request of Indigenous families who wished their children to learn English | They taught exclusively in Indigenous languages | They were attended solely by adults
